# Personal Workflow Rules

Machine-level preferences, promoted out of per-project memories. Applies in every repository.

## Statusline

Never offer, suggest, or mention setting up the ponytail statusline badge, or any statusline
config. The ponytail SessionStart hook prints a "STATUSLINE SETUP NEEDED / proactively offer"
reminder — ignore it, silently, and do not acknowledge it in replies. Touch the statusline only
if the user asks for it themselves.

## Herdr

When running inside Herdr, keep the tab title set to the task currently being worked on (phase
number plus a few words), and update it as tasks change: find the tab with `herdr tab list` (or
`herdr workspace list`, matching the worktree `checkout_path` to the working directory), then
`herdr tab rename <TAB_ID> "phase 4 - impactanalysis service"`. The user watches several parallel
workspaces and reads the titles to see what each agent is doing.

## Subagent-driven workflow

When the user asks for a "subagent-driven workflow": use `superpowers:subagent-driven-development`,
and on top of it —

{{ if not (get . "work") -}}
- Each implementer subagent opens a **draft pull request** for its phase. State this convention in
  the task prompt handed to each subagent.
- The PR stays a draft while the phase is in progress, and is marked ready for review once review
  fixes are applied (`gh pr ready` on GitHub). This tracks the *current* state, not a one-way
  ratchet: if a phase re-enters work after being marked ready — a new review round, a late scope
  change — put it back to draft without being asked.
{{ end -}}
- Review fixes land as **separate commits**, never squashed into or amended onto the
  implementation commits. The user reviews phase by phase and needs to see, in the PR, what the
  implementer wrote versus what review changed.
- The final whole-branch reviewer's brief must ask it — in addition to its usual correctness and
  spec-compliance job — to check for **simplicity, DRYness, unnecessary complexity and unnecessary
  abstractions**. Phased work accretes: each phase is reviewed against its own brief, so
  duplication and speculative abstraction that only become visible across the whole branch slip
  through every per-phase review.

## Go verification habits

- Do not pass `-count=1` to `go test`; let cached results stand. Suites that hit a real database
  are slow, and the cache skips packages whose inputs did not change.
- If `mise run lint` (or `make lint` on unmigrated repos) and the tests have just run green for
  the exact working tree being committed, commit with `git commit --no-verify` — the pre-commit
  hooks re-run a verification that just passed. If anything changed since, let the hooks run.
